course: dictionary
lesson: 1903
title: Colour: a word with two jobs
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Colour can be a noun or a verb. As a noun, colour is what you see as red, blue, green or yellow. «Stars have different colours: the hottest are blue-white, our Sun is yellow-white, and cooler stars are orange or red.» Here colour names the shades that stars shine in.
explain: As a verb, to colour means to fill in a picture with crayons, pencils or paint. Look at the words around it to see which job it is doing. If you can add «the» in front, colour is probably a noun.
explain: Colour is also the British spelling. In American English the same word is spelled color. Both spellings mean the same thing.
explain: In a list of adjectives, colour has its own place. «A lovely little old round red French wooden toy box» puts red after the size, age and shape words, just before the place it comes from. Read the sentence aloud and listen for the order.
fact: Colour is what you see as red, blue, green, yellow and so on.
fact: To colour means to fill in a picture with crayons, pencils or paint.
fact: Colour is the British way to spell color.
fact: In art, the primary colours are red, yellow and blue.
fact: «Colours opposite each other on the wheel, like orange and blue, make each other shout.»
exercise: «Stars have different colours: the hottest are blue-white, our Sun is yellow-white.» What is colour being used to mean here? | *What you see as red, blue, yellow and so on | The British spelling of color | To fill in a picture with paint | A group of things sorted by size
exercise: In «a lovely little old round red French wooden toy box», which word tells you the colour? | toy | *red | old | round
exercise: Mia will colour the shapes in red and blue. What does colour mean here? | What you see as red or blue | To sort things into groups | The way you spell a word | *To fill in with crayons or paint
test: Which is the British spelling of color? | *colour | colore | colur | colr
test: What do you get when you add white to a colour? | a shadow | a shade | a primary colour | *a tint
exam: Why are bright colours useful on a coat or a bike? | They keep you dry in the rain | *They help drivers see you | They make you run faster | They make the bike lighter
